<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andromachus of Tauromenium</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 358–344 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andromachus was the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taormina">Tauromenium</a>, modern Taormina, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in the mid-4th century BCE and the father of the historian Timaeus. In 358 BCE he gathered the survivors of Naxos, destroyed by Dionysius I, and settled them in the new city. He welcomed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timoleon">Timoleon</a> of Corinth in 344 BCE and supported his campaign to free the Greek cities of Sicily from tyrants.</p></div>
